++Freelancer Project Picker — Product Blueprint v2.0
++
++Product-first, source-validated, implementation-ready plan
++
++Prepared: 5 November 2026
++Repository: https://github.com/parasherdhruv/Freelancer-project-picker
++Document status: Product and architecture plan; not an implementation report
++Audience: Product owner, coding agent, senior engineer, technical reviewer
++
++> **Important status:** This plan does not claim that any code has been changed, any branch has been created, any API credentials have been obtained, or any provider integration has been tested end-to-end. Public documentation was reviewed on 5 November 2026. API access, terms, schemas and quotas can change; re-check official docs and run a live contract test before enabling each connector.
++
++────────
++
++0. Executive decision
++
++Build a personal Freelance Opportunity Intelligence Workbench, not a generic remote-job aggregator and not an auto-bidding bot.
++
++Its job is to help one engineer consistently answer five questions:
++
++1. What real paid projects can I apply to?
++2. Which ones are actually suitable for my skills, portfolio, location and availability?
++3. Is the client/project credible enough to spend time on?
++4. What is the strongest truthful proposal I can send?
++5. What happened after I applied, and what should I do next?
++
++The product must not pretend that a remote full-time vacancy is a freelance project. It will support two separate opportunity tracks inside one dashboard:
++
++• Freelance projects: client-posted fixed-price/hourly projects and scoped deliverables.
++• Remote roles: contract, part-time and full-time jobs from remote job boards.
++
++They share profile/evidence and parts of matching infrastructure, but they have separate source adapters, labels, filters, scoring weights and application workflows.
++
++The first release is useful even if marketplace APIs are unavailable: it can ingest public feeds for remote roles and accept user-submitted project URLs/briefs from freelance marketplaces. Marketplace API integration is an enhancement gated by documented access, not a dependency that blocks the MVP.
++
++Product success metric
++
++Do not optimize for number of scraped records. Optimize for:
++
++• relevant opportunities surfaced per week;
++• percentage of surfaced opportunities the user shortlists;
++• verified-open opportunities at the time user reviews them;
++• proposals the user actually submits;
++• replies/interviews/wins attributable to tracked opportunities;
++• time saved per qualified application.
++
++North-star metric: qualified opportunities that lead to a user-approved application, not total records ingested.
++
++────────
++
++1. Product positioning and non-goals
++
++1.1 Target user
++
++One independent software engineer based in India, with a verified profile initially describing 4 years of MERN/full-stack experience and strengths in React, Next.js, TypeScript, Node.js, NestJS, Express, MongoDB, PostgreSQL and GraphQL.
++
++These are initial seed values from prior profile context, not immutable truths. The user must be able to edit and confirm every profile fact, availability, rate, capacity, portfolio entry and location preference.
++
++1.2 Product promise
++
++“Find the best legitimate opportunities for my actual capabilities, explain why they fit, prepare an evidence-backed application, and keep me in control.”
++
++1.3 Explicit non-goals for MVP
++
++• No automatic bidding, proposal submission, messaging, or account actions.
++• No CAPTCHA solving, stealth browser automation, session-cookie harvesting, or bot-detection bypass.
++• No scraping a marketplace unless its current terms and technical rules explicitly permit the intended use.
++• No claim that an API is available just because a platform has a developer portal.
++• No multi-tenant SaaS, billing, team workspaces, Kubernetes or microservices.
++• No mandatory paid LLM.
++• No fabricated client trust metrics, salary, budget, project history, user achievements or outcomes.
++• No automatic inference that a listing is open merely because its URL returns HTTP 200.
++
++────────
++
++2. Product tracks: separate the two markets
++
++Track A — Freelance Projects
++
++Examples:
++
++• Build a Next.js dashboard.
++• Fix a React/Node production issue.
++• Develop a MERN marketplace.
++• Integrate a payment gateway.
++• Optimize a WordPress site.
++• Build an API or admin panel.
++
++Required source fields include project brief, budget/rate, client/platform, posted time, project status, proposal/bid URL, required skills and source ID when available.
++
++Primary workflow:
++Discovered → Qualified → Shortlisted → Proposal Draft → Reviewed → User Submitted → Client Reply → Interview/Discussion → Won/Lost/Withdrawn
++
++Track B — Remote Roles
++
++Examples:
++
++• Remote React Engineer.
++• Full-time Next.js developer.
++• 6-month Node.js contract.
++• Part-time technical role.
++
++Required fields include employer, employment type, salary/rate, location restrictions, timezone, seniority, application link and source attribution.
++
++Primary workflow:
++Discovered → Eligible → Shortlisted → Resume/Proposal Tailored → Reviewed → User Applied → Recruiter Reply → Interview → Offer/Rejected/Withdrawn
++
++Hard rule: A source listing tagged “contract” is not automatically a freelance marketplace project. Preserve the provider’s original employment type and store our classification separately with confidence and explanation.
++
++────────
++
++3. Source feasibility: what can actually supply opportunities?
++
++This is the most important section. A provider is not “integrated” until we have confirmed: official access, allowed use, the relevant record type, fields, pagination, quotas, freshness, attribution, application URL, authentication and a passing live contract test.
++
++3.1 Source tiers
++
++Tier 1 — Public documented feeds: build first, but only for Remote Roles
++
++|Provider |What it supplies                                                                                 |Auth                                                               |Freshness / limits                                                                                                                                   |Decision                                                                    |
++|---------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
++|Himalayas|Remote jobs; employment type includes contract/full-time/part-time                               |Public API works without key; optional free read key               |API docs say data refreshes every 24h; browse max 20/page with cursor; free key quota documented as 60/min and 10,000/day                            |Build as Remote Roles adapter                                               |
++|Jobicy   |Remote jobs, including job type categories; its current docs include freelance/contract job types|Public API no key; optional commercial API key for direct ATS links|Current docs describe last-7-day public feed, cursor pagination, max 100/page; commercial direct links can cost $0.01 per newly returned eligible job|Build as Remote Roles adapter; do not enable paid commercial mode by default|
++|Remote OK|Remote tech job JSON and RSS feeds                                                               |Public feed; no auth                                               |Follow current FAQ/fair-use guidance; cache and attribute source                                                                                     |Build as Remote Roles adapter                                               |
++
++Important source correction: These providers are not equivalent to Upwork/Freelancer.com client project marketplaces. Even when a listing says “freelance” or “contract”, it is still a job-board listing, not necessarily an open client project accepting competitive bids.
++
++Tier 2 — Marketplace API candidates: feasibility gate before coding
++
++|Provider                      |Publicly confirmed facts                                                             |Unknown / must verify                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |MVP decision                                                                           |
++|------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
++|Freelancer.com                |Official Freelancer API developer portal exists and advertises API docs and a sandbox|Whether the exact current API exposes project discovery to a freelancer account; required auth, account role, scopes, rate limits, approval, and permission for this personal opportunity-discovery use                                                                           |First marketplace API feasibility spike; do not assume access                          |
++|Upwork                        |Official GraphQL API and OAuth 2.0 docs exist; API key requests are reviewed         |Current API docs list eligibility criteria including verified profile/payment, identity verification, account standing, and at least $25,000 lifetime earnings/spend or combined, plus 90% JSS for freelancers/agencies; actual job-search scope and permitted use must be checked|Treat as blocked/approval-gated until account eligibility and exact scope are confirmed|
++|Contra                        |Platform exists for independent work                                                 |No confirmed general-purpose public project-search API contract in this review                                                                                                                                                                                                    |Manual URL intake only until official API/partner access is documented                 |
++|Fiverr                        |Marketplace exists                                                                   |No confirmed public buyer-request/project-search API suitable for this use                                                                                                                                                                                                        |Manual URL intake only                                                                 |
++|PeoplePerHour / Guru / similar|Marketplace opportunities may exist                                                  |API access and automation permissions not verified here                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |Do not build connector without official evidence                                       |
++
++Critical distinction: “There is a developer API” does not prove “this API lets a freelancer search open client projects and use it for a personal job-hunting agent.” Test that exact user journey.
++
++Tier 3 — User-provided/manual opportunity intake: guaranteed MVP path
++
++Support:
++
++• Paste project URL.
++• Paste project description/brief.
++• Enter budget, skills and deadline if visible.
++• Upload or paste a screenshot/text excerpt where platform terms allow.
++• Select source platform and mark whether login is required.
++• Save original URL and user-entered metadata.
++• Let user confirm any extracted fields before scoring.
++
++The system should never log into a marketplace or fetch protected pages on the user’s behalf unless a documented permitted integration exists.
++
++Tier 4 — Direct client leads: later, separate lead type
++
++Potential sources include company career pages, public RFPs, startup communities, founder posts and user-forwarded emails. These are leads, not automatically verified paid projects. Add only after source-specific permission and provenance are defined. Do not turn social-media scraping into a hidden dependency.
++
++3.2 Source acceptance checklist
++
++A source cannot move to “enabled” until all are true:
++
++1. Official docs or written permission found.
++2. Terms allow this use case and display/storage/analysis.
++3. Correct user role can access the data.
++4. Data includes a stable ID or reliable canonical URL.
++5. Required fields are available or explicitly nullable.
++6. Pagination and rate limits are understood.
++7. Application/bid link is known and must be preserved.
++8. Attribution is implemented.
++9. Expiry/status semantics are understood.
++10. Live contract test passes with a small request.
++11. Failure and 429 behavior are tested.
++12. Source owner/terms URL and last-reviewed date are recorded.
++
++If a condition is unknown, source status is FEASIBILITY_PENDING, not ACTIVE.
++
++3.3 Provider-specific integration notes
++
++Himalayas
++
++• Official docs: https://himalayas.app/api
++• API reference: https://himalayas.app/docs/remote-jobs-api
++• OpenAPI: https://himalayas.app/docs/openapi.json
++• Browse: GET https://himalayas.app/jobs/api?limit=20
++• Search: GET https://himalayas.app/jobs/api/search?...
++• Single listing status: GET /jobs/api/companies/{companySlug}/jobs/{slug}
++• Cursor pagination is preferred; max 20 per browse request.
++• No key required. Optional free API key raises documented account quota to 60 requests/minute and 10,000/day.
++• Data refreshes every 24 hours. Do not poll more often just to simulate real-time freshness.
++• 200 means open; 410 means closed; 404 means not found/unavailable.
++• Every Apply action must use the API’s applicationLink unchanged.
++• Show visible Himalayas attribution and link. Do not pass their listings to third-party job aggregators or Google Jobs/LinkedIn Jobs feeds/structured data.
++• Backend calls only; browser CORS is not enabled.
++
++Jobicy
++
++• Official docs: https://jobicy.com/jobs-rss-feed
++• Official API repo/OpenAPI: https://github.com/Jobicy/remote-jobs-api
++• Current docs reviewed on 5 Nov 2026 describe public GET https://jobicy.com/api/jobs, no key, last-7-day listings, cursor pagination and up to 100 per page.
++• Public url is the Jobicy listing URL. An optional commercial API key can return a direct ATS application URL where available; current docs describe a $0.01 charge for each newly returned job with an available direct URL, per API key. Do not enable this for the MVP.
++• The current docs also describe a batch status endpoint for up to 100 IDs and active, closed, unknown states.
++• The Jobicy docs/site have shown different endpoint/version paths over time. Therefore implementation must fetch and pin the current official OpenAPI schema at development time; do not copy an old endpoint from a blog, cached README or previous implementation.
++• Retain Jobicy attribution and original listing URL.
++• Sanitize HTML descriptions.
++• Poll a few times per day; do not exceed the provider’s stated hourly minimum interval.
++
++Remote OK
++
++• Official FAQ: https://remoteok.com/faq
++• JSON: GET https://remoteok.com/api
++• RSS: https://remoteok.com/remote-jobs.rss
++• Tag filtering is available (e.g. ?tag=dev).
++• Public feed; no authentication required.
++• Attribute Remote OK and link original listing.
++• Treat listings as remote jobs, not freelance projects by default.
++• Use only salary/rate fields actually supplied; missing values remain unknown.
++
++Freelancer.com
++
++• Official developer portal: https://developers.freelancer.com/
++• Portal advertises official API documentation and a sandbox.
++• A real API exists, but this review has not verified that the available endpoints permit the exact freelancer-side project search flow needed here.
++• Feasibility spike must establish: API key/application registration, authentication/signing method, project search endpoint, project details, bid eligibility, account role, sandbox behavior, rate limits and terms.
++• Do not infer OAuth or scopes until the current API docs specify them.
++• If the API does not permit the desired use, keep manual URL intake.
++
++Upwork
++
++• Developer portal/docs: https://www.upwork.com/developer/
++• GraphQL API docs: https://www.upwork.com/developer/documentation/graphql/api/docs/index.html
++• Auth guidance: https://support.upwork.com/hc/en-us/articles/17995930500883–API-authentication-and-security
++• OAuth 2.0 is required for API requests.
++• Current public docs describe a reviewed API-key application and account eligibility conditions, including identity/profile/payment checks and lifetime earnings/spend threshold. Verify current requirements in the account before planning this connector.
++• Do not assume that OAuth approval implies job-search permission. Confirm the exact GraphQL query, permission and intended-use approval.
++• Upwork API is an optional future integration, not an MVP dependency.
++
++────────
++
++4. Product discovery validation before implementation
++
++Before building the full product, run a 3–5 day validation sprint.
++
++Experiment A — Real opportunity supply
++
++• Collect 30–50 opportunities manually from the target freelance marketplaces and 30–50 from remote job feeds.
++• Label each by source, type, freshness, India eligibility, budget, skill fit and apply route.
++• Separate real client projects from employment roles.
++• Measure how many are genuinely actionable for this profile.
++
++Experiment B — Source/API feasibility
++
++• Test the public APIs with low request volume.
++• Inspect official schemas and sample payloads.
++• Test pagination, duplicates, missing salary, HTML content, status checks and 429 handling.
++• Submit/inspect developer API access requirements for Freelancer.com; inspect Upwork eligibility and query scopes.
++• Record blockers with evidence; do not invent a workaround.
++
++Experiment C — Ranking usefulness
++
++• Manually label 50 opportunities: strong fit, possible fit, poor fit, invalid/expired.
++• Run a simple baseline matcher.
++• Compare the top 10 ranked results with the human labels.
++• Tune weights only after observing false positives/false negatives.
++
++Validation exit criteria
++
++• At least one reliable source for each enabled track.
++• At least 20 genuine actionable freelance projects or a documented conclusion that marketplace API supply is unavailable and manual intake is the MVP route.
++• At least 70% precision in the top 10 shortlist after human review on the test set (initial target, not a claimed result).
++• No fabricated trust, salary, budget or source data.
++• Source terms and attribution recorded.
++
++If validation fails, narrow the product scope before writing the full platform.
