+11. Data model and lifecycle
+
+Design schema from workflows, not from an arbitrary list of entities.
+
+Core entities:
+
+• User (single owner in MVP; retain ownership field for future isolation).
+• FreelancerProfile.
+• Skill and ProfileSkill (or normalized skill JSON initially).
+• PortfolioProject and PortfolioEvidence.
+• SourceRegistry.
+• SourceCredential (encrypted, optional, provider-specific).
+• DiscoveryRun.
+• Opportunity.
+• OpportunitySourceReference (allows the same canonical opportunity to be observed from multiple sources).
+• OpportunitySnapshot.
+• OpportunityVerification.
+• OpportunityAnalysis.
+• MatchEvaluation.
+• Proposal and ProposalVersion.
+• Application.
+• ApplicationEvent.
+• UserFeedback.
+• AuditEvent.
+
+Opportunity key strategy:
+
+1. (sourceId, externalId) where stable provider ID exists.
+2. Canonical URL unique per source.
+3. Cross-source fingerprint as a candidate duplicate, not automatic merge unless confidence is high.
+4. Preserve every source reference and original link.
+
+Important fields:
+
+• opportunityType: FREELANCE_PROJECT / CONTRACT_ROLE / FULL_TIME_ROLE / PART_TIME_ROLE / UNKNOWN.
+• sourceStatus: provider’s raw status.
+• availabilityStatus: OPEN / CLOSED / UNKNOWN / NEEDS_RECHECK.
+• clientRating, reviewCount, spend, paymentVerified: nullable.
+• budgetMin, budgetMax, hourlyMin, hourlyMax: nullable with currency and period.
+• firstSeenAt, lastSeenAt, lastVerifiedAt, postedAt, expiresAt.
+• sourcePayloadHash, not unlimited raw payload retention.
+• classificationConfidence, analysisConfidence.
+
+────────
+
+12. Security, privacy and compliance
+
+• Owner-only authentication for every private route.
+• Secure HttpOnly/SameSite cookies, CSRF protection, login rate limits.
+• Strict CORS allowlist.
+• DTO/schema validation.
+• Encrypted OAuth/API tokens at rest.
+• Secrets only in deployment secret manager.
+• Audit changes, approvals, integration connect/disconnect.
+• Least-privilege database access.
+• No marketplace passwords stored.
+• Clear delete/disconnect workflow.
+• Do not retain raw resume or email data unless required and user-consented.
+
+SSRF controls for manual URL intake
+
+• Accept only http/https, preferably https.
+• Restrict automatic fetch to known allowed domains.
+• Reject localhost, loopback, private, link-local, multicast and reserved IP ranges.
+• Resolve DNS and validate every resolved address; defend against DNS rebinding.
+• Disable redirects or revalidate every redirect destination.
+• Limit timeout, response size, content type and ports.
+• Never fetch arbitrary URLs from privileged internal network.
+• For MVP, safest path is user-pasted text/metadata plus opening the original URL in the user’s browser.
+
+Platform compliance rules
+
+• Official API or permission only.
+• Preserve attribution and original application URLs.
+• Respect quotas, cache instructions, deletion/expiry rules and robots/terms where relevant.
+• Do not evade access controls.
+• Do not store cookies or passwords to imitate user sessions.
+• Record source terms URL, reviewed date and permitted use in SourceRegistry.
+
+────────
+
+13. Cloud execution and reliability
+
+The PC must not be required to run scheduled discovery.
+
+Recommended MVP:
+
+• Railway web service.
+• Railway API service.
+• One Railway worker/scheduler process.
+• Managed PostgreSQL.
+• Redis only if queue/locking needs justify it.
+
+Reliability requirements:
+
+• Durable DiscoveryRun records.
+• Idempotent upserts.
+• Single-run lock per provider/schedule.
+• Retry with exponential backoff and jitter.
+• Respect Retry-After.
+• Provider circuit breaker and health status.
+• Worker heartbeat and missed-run alert.
+• Never interpret provider outage as an empty feed.
+• Database-backed queue or durable job state for important tasks.
+• Graceful shutdown and retry-safe jobs.
+
+Suggested schedules:
+
+• Himalayas: once daily (data refresh is daily).
+• Jobicy: 2–4 times daily, respecting minimum interval and current docs.
+• Remote OK: 2–4 times daily subject to current fair use.
+• Marketplace sources: only documented quota and access permit; start with conservative sync.
+• Manual sync: user-triggered with rate limit.
+
+Do not assume Railway free-tier services are always-on. Verify current pricing/sleep/usage limits before choosing the hosting plan. Display last successful sync and scheduler heartbeat in the UI.
+
+────────
+
+14. MVP scope and phased delivery
+
+Phase 0 — Product validation and source feasibility (highest priority)
+
+Deliver:
+
+• 3–5 day validation report.
+• Source matrix with official docs, terms, record types, access, fields, quotas and blockers.
+• Sample opportunity dataset labelled by human.
+• Manual intake prototype specification.
+• Upwork and Freelancer.com feasibility decisions.
+Exit:
+• At least one valid path to real freelance project records (marketplace API if approved, otherwise user-assisted manual intake).
+• Remote role feeds clearly separate.
+• No unverified source treated as live.
+
+Phase 1 — Data integrity and domain separation
+
+Deliver:
+
+• Separate freelance project and remote role domain types.
+• Remove fabricated seed/live opportunities and positive trust defaults.
+• Null unknown values.
+• Database as sole production source of truth.
+• Durable source provenance and dedup.
+Exit:
+• No fabricated opportunities or trust metrics.
+• Repeat sync is idempotent.
+• Database failure is visible.
+
+Phase 2 — Manual intake and real source adapters
+
+Deliver:
+
+• Secure manual URL/text intake.
+• Himalayas, Jobicy, Remote OK remote-role adapters.
+• Freelancer.com adapter only if source gate passes.
+• Upwork adapter only after account/API approval and scope confirmation.
+Exit:
+• Each active adapter has contract tests and attribution.
+• Manual intake works when marketplace APIs are unavailable.
+
+Phase 3 — Portfolio intelligence
+
+Deliver:
+
+• Verified portfolio evidence library.
+• Skills/evidence taxonomy.
+• Opportunity-to-evidence mapping.
+Exit:
+• Every proposal claim can be traced to user-confirmed evidence.
+
+Phase 4 — Opportunity analysis and matching
+
+Deliver:
+
+• Classification and confidence.
+• Separate scoring models for freelance projects and remote roles.
+• Budget/effective-rate analysis.
+• Risk flags and explainability.
+Exit:
+• Human-labelled test set demonstrates useful top-10 precision; target ≥70% initially.
+
+Phase 5 — Proposal engine
+
+Deliver:
+
+• Templates by project category.
+• Evidence selection.
+• Deterministic proposal generation.
+• Optional LLM adapter.
+• Claim validation and review.
+Exit:
+• No unsupported factual claims in test corpus.
+• User must approve every proposal.
+
+Phase 6 — Application tracking and dashboard
+
+Deliver:
+
+• Separate Freelance Projects and Remote Roles views.
+• Shortlist, proposal, application and outcome tracking.
+• Source health and run history.
+Exit:
+• End-to-end journey passes on real sample opportunities.
+
+Phase 7 — Cloud hardening and release
+
+Deliver:
+
+• Auth, SSRF controls, CORS, rate limits, audit.
+• Worker reliability and monitoring.
